HTTP Request Smuggling exploits discrepancies between how frontend reverse proxies (e.g., Cloudflare, NGINX, HAProxy) and backend application servers (e.g., Go net/http) delimit the boundaries of piped HTTP requests across a shared persistent TCP connection. CL.TE vs. TE.CL Desync CL.TE: Frontend honors Content-Length, forwarding the entire body. Backend honors Transfer-Encoding: chunked, terminates parsing early at the zero chunk (0

), and treats the remainder of the body as the start of the next incoming request on that TCP socket. TE.CL: Frontend processes chunked streams. Backend reads only Content-Length bytes, leaving the rest of the chunked payload queued in the TCP buffer to poison the next victim's request. HTTP/2 Downgrading (H2.CL / H2.TE) When frontend proxies accept HTTP/2 binary frames and downgrade them to HTTP/1.1 keep-alive connections to backend Go servers, header injection becomes possible: An attacker sends an HTTP/2 header containing CRLF: foo: bar
Transfer-Encoding: chunked. When translated to HTTP/1.1 plaintext on the backend connection, this splits the HTTP stream and poisons the backend connection pool. Defense: Configure frontend proxies to use e

Inside Go's standard library (net/http/server.go): The Commit Point: When w.WriteHeader(code) or the first w.Write(bytes) executes, Go inspects w.wroteHeader. If false, it marks wroteHeader = true, writes the status line (HTTP/1.1 200 OK
), formats the header map into response.bufw ( bufio.Writer), and appends the trailing CRLF (

). Post-Commit Silent Drop: If subsequent application code or deferred middleware calls w.Header().Set("X-Security", "true"), Go modifies the in-memory map, but response.Write never re-serializes headers. The added headers are completely invisible on the wire.
